<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hexamilion wall</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hexamilion was a defensive wall about six Roman miles long across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isthmus-of-corinth">Isthmus of Corinth</a>, guarding the only land route into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>. It was built in the early 5th century CE under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii">Theodosius II</a> and repaired several times. The Byzantine emperor Manuel II rebuilt it in 1415 against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, but Ottoman armies broke through in 1423 and 1446.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hexamilion-wall.jpg" alt="Hexamilion wall" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nefasdicere · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hexamilion-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Attempts to fortify the isthmus go back perhaps to the Mycenaean period. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> invaded in 480 BCE many Peloponnesian cities wanted to hold the isthmus rather than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a>, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> noted that such a wall was useless without control of the sea. The Hexamilion was built between 408 and 450, in the age of the great barbarian invasions, and the attack of Alaric on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in 396 or the sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 410 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> may have prompted it. Under Justinian it gained more towers, 153 in all, with forts at either end and the fortress at Isthmia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Running from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-corinth">Gulf of Corinth</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saronic-gulf">Saronic Gulf</a>, the wall was about 7.5 kilometres long, 7 metres high and 3 metres thick, a rubble and mortar core faced with squared stones. It is the largest archaeological structure in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, and its builders took stone from older buildings: the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a> at Isthmia was built into it, while the sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a> at Perachora and much of the ancient statuary of Corinth were burned for lime. The fortress of Isthmia, with nineteen towers over 2.7 hectares, probably housed the garrison, and its north-east gate was the main way into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Local farmers first manned the wall until Justinian replaced them with a professional garrison, and the gates allowed trade to be taxed. Locals seem often to have resisted it: graves were dug at the foot of a key staircase, and the gates were later sealed with hasty walls. Military use fell off after the 7th century, and earthquakes, above all that of 551, damaged it. When Manuel II rebuilt it in 1415 an open revolt had to be put down by force. After Constantine Palaiologos and his brother Thomas restored it in 1444, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a>'s army reduced it to ruins on 10 December 1446. It never fulfilled its purpose, though it may have deterred attack.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hexamilion wall” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Hexamilion wall</b> across the Isthmus was rebuilt in 1415 against the Ottomans.</blockquote><a href="/state/despotate-of-the-morea">Despotate of the Morea</a></li></ul></section>
